<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    QuickAuth React Native SDK — Android manifest.

    No `package="…"` attribute. AGP 8 dropped support for it — "Setting the namespace via the
    package attribute in the source AndroidManifest.xml is no longer supported" — and a library
    manifest that still declares one fails the consuming app's build on the early AGP 8 releases
    that the React Native 0.73 and 0.74 templates ship. Later 8.x ignores it with a warning, so
    the symptom depended on which template a merchant started from. Since every current React
    Native template is on AGP 8, this one attribute was the difference between the SDK building
    and not. The namespace lives in android/build.gradle, where AGP wants it, and is the same
    value it always was: io.quickauth.rnsdk.

    No permissions either. This manifest used to declare RECEIVE_SMS, which the SDK has never
    needed — Google's SMS Retriever delivers OTP messages via a system broadcast scoped to the
    receiving app's hash, precisely so an app does not have to ask for SMS access. A library
    manifest merges into every app that depends on it, and RECEIVE_SMS is in Google Play's
    restricted set: an app declaring it must justify the use in a Play Console declaration or be
    removed from the store. Declaring one the SDK never uses created a review problem for every
    merchant in exchange for nothing. INTERNET is likewise dropped; every React Native app
    already declares it, and a library forcing it in hides that from the app that actually needs
    it.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <!--
        Android 11 (API 30) filters which other apps a package can see, and that filtering
        applies to an explicit broadcast just as it does to a query: setPackage("com.whatsapp")
        on a device where WhatsApp is invisible to us delivers nothing.

        The OTP_REQUESTED handshake in QuickAuthSmsRetrieverModule is exactly that kind of
        broadcast, so without this an app targeting API 30+ — which is every app now — silently
        fails to hand WhatsApp the handshake, and zero-tap cannot work no matter how correct the
        template, the signing hash and the receiver are. Nothing throws; the broadcast is simply
        dropped.

        Both packages, because the user's code arrives on whichever WhatsApp they have. Mirrors
        WHATSAPP_PACKAGES in QuickAuthSmsRetrieverModule; change the two together.
    -->
    <queries>
        <package android:name="com.whatsapp" />
        <package android:name="com.whatsapp.w4b" />
    </queries>

    <application>
        <!--
            WhatsApp delivers a zero-tap or one-tap code by broadcasting to the app, not by SMS,
            so Google's SmsRetriever never sees it. Declared statically rather than registered at
            runtime because zero-tap's whole promise is that the user does nothing: the code can
            arrive while the app is backgrounded or not running, and a runtime receiver would
            only exist when the app already does.

            No android:permission, deliberately. It is tempting to restrict the sender, and
            guessing at a WhatsApp-owned permission name would be the worst mistake available
            here, because Android silently refuses to deliver a broadcast to a receiver guarded by
            a permission the sender does not hold. The receiver would never fire, nothing would
            throw, and the symptom would be "zero-tap does not work" with no error to chase.
            Meta's documented receiver declares exported="true" and no permission.

            What keeps this safe is WhatsApp's own check, not ours: it only broadcasts to an app
            whose package name AND 11-character signing hash match the ones on the approved
            template. An attacker cannot satisfy that without the merchant's signing key. The
            receiver additionally rejects anything that is not a plausible code.

            The action string is duplicated in WhatsAppOtpReceiver.ACTION_OTP_RETRIEVED — a
            manifest cannot reference a Java constant. Change both together.
        -->
        <receiver
            android:name="io.quickauth.rnsdk.WhatsAppOtpReceiver"
            android:enabled="true"
            android:exported="true">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="com.whatsapp.otp.OTP_RETRIEVED" />
            </intent-filter>
        </receiver>
    </application>

</manifest>
